BT /F1 10 Tf 40 760 Td 13 TL (Chapter 6: Nutrition in Plants \(class notes\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.1 What is photosynthesis?) Tj T* (Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants make their own food. The plant takes) Tj T* (in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny pores called stomata and absorbs water from) Tj T* (the soil through its roots. Using the energy of sunlight, the plant combines carbon) Tj T* (dioxide and water to produce glucose, a simple sugar, which it uses as food. The word) Tj T* (equation is: carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen, in the presence of sunlight and) Tj T* (chlorophyll. Glucose that is not used at once is stored as starch.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.2 Chlorophyll and chloroplasts) Tj T* (The green colour of leaves comes from a pigment called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is found) Tj T* (inside small structures in the leaf cells called chloroplasts. Chlorophyll traps light) Tj T* (energy from the sun, which is why photosynthesis takes place mainly in the leaves. Leaves) Tj T* (are broad and flat so that they can capture as much light as possible. Plants without) Tj T* (chlorophyll, such as fungi, cannot photosynthesise and must obtain food in other ways.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.3 Oxygen, the by-product) Tj T* (During photosynthesis oxygen is released as a by-product. The oxygen leaves the plant) Tj T* (through the stomata and enters the air. This is the source of the oxygen that animals and) Tj T* (humans breathe. A simple experiment with a water plant such as Hydrilla shows bubbles of) Tj T* (oxygen gas being given off when the plant is kept in sunlight. The gas collected in the) Tj T* (test tube relights a glowing splint, which proves that it is oxygen.) Tj T* ET
