BT /F1 10 Tf 40 760 Td 13 TL (6.4 Why plants need water) Tj T* (Water is one of the raw materials of photosynthesis, so a plant cannot make food without) Tj T* (it. Water also carries dissolved mineral salts such as nitrates from the soil up to the) Tj T* (leaves. Water keeps the plant cells firm, or turgid, which keeps the stem upright and the) Tj T* (leaves spread out; a plant short of water wilts. Finally, water that evaporates from the) Tj T* (leaves in transpiration cools the plant on hot days.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.5 Stomata and gas exchange) Tj T* (Stomata are tiny openings on the surface of leaves, mostly on the lower side. Each stoma) Tj T* (is surrounded by two guard cells that open and close it. Through the stomata the leaf) Tj T* (takes in carbon dioxide and gives out oxygen and water vapour. Stomata usually open in) Tj T* (daylight and close at night to reduce water loss.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.6 Conditions needed for photosynthesis) Tj T* (Photosynthesis needs four things: sunlight, chlorophyll, carbon dioxide and water. The) Tj T* (rate of photosynthesis increases with more light and more carbon dioxide up to a limit,) Tj T* (and it is also affected by temperature. A variegated leaf, which has white patches without) Tj T* (chlorophyll, tested with iodine shows starch only in the green parts, proving that) Tj T* (chlorophyll is needed.) Tj T* () Tj T* (6.7 Other modes of nutrition) Tj T* (Some plants cannot make their own food. Cuscuta \(dodder\) is a parasite that takes ready-) Tj T* (made food from the host plant it climbs on. The pitcher plant is insectivorous: insects) Tj T* (trapped in its pitcher are digested to supply nitrogen. Fungi such as mushrooms are) Tj T* (saprotrophs that feed on dead and decaying matter. Lichens are a partnership between an) Tj T* (alga, which photosynthesises, and a fungus, which provides shelter and water.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
endstream
